  "hints": [
    "How does a tree's height relate to the heights of its two subtrees?",
    "height(node) = 1 + max(height(left), height(right)), with height(null) = 0.",
    "Without recursion, run a breadth-first search and count how many levels you process."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function treeHeight(root) {\n  let level = root ? [root] : []\n  let height = 0\n  while (level.length) {\n    height++\n    const next = []\n    for (const node of level) {\n      if (node.left) next.push(node.left)\n      if (node.right) next.push(node.right)\n    }\n    level = next\n  }\n  return height\n}\n",
    "py": "def tree_height(root):\n    level = [root] if root else []\n    height = 0\n    while level:\n        height += 1\n        level = [child for node in level for child in (node[\"left\"], node[\"right\"]) if child]\n    return height\n",
    "explanation": "Process the tree one level at a time; the number of non-empty levels is the height. This iterative BFS avoids the call-stack depth limits that a recursive solution hits on very deep, list-like trees."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(w)",
    "notes": "w is the maximum width of a level. The recursive version uses O(h) stack space instead."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "When would you prefer the recursive version, and how deep can it go before overflowing?"
  ]
}
